---
/**
 * /architecture — THE THIRD SHELF (Huayin + CG2, 2026-08-30).
 *
 * WHY IT EXISTS. The rail was Foundations · Analytical Governance · Doors · Columna — a layer, a
 * single paper's doorway, a reader-entry surface, and a system. Those are not four of a kind.
 * `/analytical-governance` says so about itself: it is the doorway to ONE deposited record, which it
 * may compress and connect but not extend. It held a rail slot because there was no shelf to put it
 * on. This page is that shelf, and Analytical Governance is now a work inside it.
 *
 * NOT A MISCELLANY, AND NOT A DESIGN-PATTERNS PAGE. The membership question is not "is this
 * theoretical?" — that would make Architecture a residual category for work Foundations did not
 * want. The works here share a subject: each governs a PASSAGE. Foundations answers what exists and
 * what laws hold. Architecture answers what may pass, and under whose authority.
 *
 * THE TEST IS A DESCRIPTION, NOT A GATE (Huayin, 2026-08-30). It is here to help write the page, not
 * to adjudicate membership. Cross-listing is fine and expected: a work may sit on two shelves
 * because a reader might look for it on either. Do not build a rule that forces a choice. The
 * Statistical Bridge is the live case — it establishes what must EXIST for inference to be
 * possible AND governs the passage from governed evidence to a defensible claim, so it appears
 * here and on /foundations, and neither placement is a demotion of the other.
 *
 * GOVERNING ACROSS LEADS, AND IS NOT FILED UNDER THREE WORLDS. Its central claim is general: if two
 * independently governed objects are merged, the relation between them stops being representable as
 * an independently governed act. That holds vertically across the three worlds and horizontally
 * between two business domains — the paper spends a third of its argument on a case where no
 * ontological boundary is crossed at all. So Three Worlds INSTANTIATES this rule; it does not imply
 * it. The reading order Three Worlds → Governing Across is pedagogy, not dependency, and the prose
 * says so rather than letting the sequence assert otherwise.
 *
 * ORDER IS A READING MOVEMENT, NOT A DEPENDENCY GRAPH. General rule, then the passage of an
 * analytical service, then what changes when an agent traverses it. No arrows, no numbering: every
 * numbered list of three is read as a pipeline, and this is not one.
 *
 * NO PUBLICATION FACT IS TYPED HERE. Titles, versions, dates and records come from the registry
 * through data/publications.ts. This is a `derived` surface under G7 and the build fails if a DOI
 * is typed into this file.
 *
 * COLUMNA IS THE NEXT ROOM, NOT THE EVIDENCE. The page ends by pointing at Columna as one system
 * that can be evaluated against the principle — general architectural principle → an implementation
 * that can be checked. Not "Columna proves this": the deduction does not depend on it.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { citation } from '../data/publications';

const gaow = citation('w-governing-across-ontological-worlds');
const ag = citation('w-analytical-governance');
const agent = citation('w-do-not-let-your-ai-agent-govern-itself');
const bridge = citation('w-statistical-bridge');
const tw = citation('w-three-worlds-of-analytics');

// One entry per crossing. `question` is what the work answers for — the governing question, in the
// reader's words, not the paper's abstract.
const CROSSINGS = [
  {
    id: 'between-worlds',
    word: 'Between worlds',
    question: 'How may independently governed objects and jurisdictions compose?',
    opens: [
      'When two systems agree they mean the same supplier, what else — if anything — has been agreed?',
      'If a business definition and its analytical identity are one object, where did the transfer of authority go?',
      'What is left of a governed relation once the architecture declares its two sides to be one thing?',
    ],
    state:
      'The claim is a negative one, and it is load-bearing: a crossing can be governed as a crossing ' +
      'only while the worlds on its two sides remain distinct. Merge them and the transfer of ' +
      'authority still happens — it simply stops being representable as a separate, challengeable act.',
    held: 'Permissionless reference is not permissionless authority.',
    heldSupport:
      'A shared identifier can be strong evidence for a relation. It does not establish the chain ' +
      'that runs from same referent through same meaning to same standing for use, because each of ' +
      'those is a separate claim that someone has to make.',
    waysIn: [
      { href: '/read/governing-across-ontological-worlds', label: gaow.label,
        hint: 'The general rule, and the three recurring ways architectures break it.' },
      { href: '/read/three-worlds-of-analytics', label: tw.label,
        hint: 'The jurisdictions this rule is most often applied to. Read first if you like — that is pedagogy, not dependency.' },
    ],
    unsettled:
      'A crossing is defined here as a governed relation — endpoints, purpose, evidence, authority, ' +
      'permitted consequences, revision. What is not settled is what CARRIES it. A declaration can ' +
      'be authoritative in one environment and become mere data when it is serialised and handed to ' +
      'another, and nothing yet says how a receiving side establishes that identity, evidence and ' +
      'standing survived the passage.',
  },
  {
    id: 'through-the-service',
    word: 'Through the service',
    question: 'When is an analytical service legitimate?',
    opens: [
      'Does the purpose a person arrived with survive being turned into a request?',
      'What must a served result carry for the answer built on it to be defensible?',
      'What should a system do when it can compute a number but cannot stand behind it?',
    ],
    state:
      'The subject is the legitimacy of the whole passage — purpose, request, result, answer, ' +
      'consequential use — rather than the correctness of any one step in it. Servability is a gate ' +
      'inside that question, not the question.',
    waysIn: [
      { href: '/analytical-governance', label: ag.label,
        hint: 'The doorway to the current record, with the worked example.' },
    ],
    unsettled:
      'Standing is the least settled part. A result that is legitimate here is not automatically ' +
      'legitimate once it crosses into another system, and what a result must carry with it to keep ' +
      'its standing is an open question rather than a solved one.',
  },
  {
    id: 'by-an-agent',
    word: 'By an agent',
    question: 'How can an intelligent agent traverse a governed system without becoming the source of its own authority?',
    opens: [
      'Which of the boundaries a person used to stand at were ever written down?',
      'What is the difference between reaching an object and being permitted to use it?',
      'If every local step is valid, what stops an upstream settlement travelling further than its grounds?',
    ],
    state:
      'AI did not create these passages. It changed the conditions under which they are traversed. ' +
      'The people at the boundaries were called translators; they were doing something else — ' +
      'carrying authority distinctions the systems themselves did not represent. An agent crosses ' +
      'all of them in one continuous motion.',
    held: 'Let intelligence traverse broadly. Let authority traverse only through governed passages.',
    heldSupport:
      'This permits more capable agents, not weaker ones. A system can safely expose more of its ' +
      'legitimate request space once reaching an object is no longer confused with authority to ' +
      'make every use of it.',
    waysIn: [
      { href: '/read/do-not-let-your-ai-agent-govern-itself', label: agent.label,
        hint: 'Authority separation applied directly to agents.' },
      { href: '/read/statistical-bridge', label: bridge.label,
        hint: 'The passage from governed evidence to a claim it licenses. Also on Foundations — it belongs to both.' },
    ],
    unsettled:
      'An agent may propose a relation that does not yet exist. What it may not do is ratify its ' +
      'own proposal. Who ratifies, on what evidence, and how quickly enough to be usable, is an ' +
      'organisational question this work states rather than answers.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Architecture — datumwise"
  description="How authority, meaning, data and execution are allowed to compose — and why the boundary at which one kind of authority becomes another has to survive the composing."
>
  <article class="arch">
    <header class="opening">
      <p class="kicker">Architecture</p>
      <h1>Architecture</h1>

      <p class="lede">
        How independently governed things compose without losing the boundary at which one kind of
        authority becomes another.
      </p>
      <p class="body">
        <a href="/foundations">Foundations</a> asks what exists and what laws hold over it. These
        questions are different: they are about <em>passage</em>. What may cross from one governed
        object to another, on whose authority, for what purpose, and with what left behind.
      </p>
      <p class="body">
        The problem is not integration. Enterprise systems have every reason to seek shared meaning,
        common identifiers and reusable metrics. The problem is where integration is made
        authoritative — because coupling that erases a boundary does not remove the transfer of
        authority across it. It removes the ability to govern that transfer as its own act.
      </p>

      <p class="terrain">Separate the worlds. Govern the crossings. Compose through the crossings.</p>
      <p class="caveat">
        Three crossings, not three stages. A result does not pass through them in order, and together
        they do not claim to cover the subject. They are three places where authority moves and where
        an architecture can quietly stop representing that it did.
      </p>
    </header>

    {CROSSINGS.map((c) => (
      <section class="crossing" id={c.id} aria-labelledby={`${c.id}-h`}>
        <hr class="rule rule-strong" />
        <h2 id={`${c.id}-h`} class="word">{c.word}</h2>
        <p class="question">{c.question}</p>

        <div class="moves">
          <div class="move">
            <h3 class="move-h">What it opens</h3>
            <ul class="opens">{c.opens.map((q) => <li>{q}</li>)}</ul>
          </div>

          <div class="move">
            <h3 class="move-h">Where we are</h3>
            <p class="body">{c.state}</p>
            {c.held && (
              <>
                <blockquote class="held">{c.held}</blockquote>
                <p class="body">{c.heldSupport}</p>
              </>
            )}
          </div>

          <div class="move">
            <h3 class="move-h">Ways in</h3>
            <ul class="ways">
              {c.waysIn.map((w) => (
                <li>
                  <a href={w.href}>{w.label}</a>
                  <span class="hint">{w.hint}</span>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="move">
            <h3 class="move-h">What is unsettled</h3>
            <p class="body">{c.unsettled}</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    ))}

    <section class="relate" aria-labelledby="relate-h">
      <hr class="rule rule-strong" />
      <h2 id="relate-h" class="word">How these relate</h2>

      <p class="body">
        One general result and two of the places it bites hardest. The general result is that merging
        independently governed objects destroys the very act you would want to hold someone to. That
        applies vertically — between business meaning, governed data and material execution — and
        horizontally, between two business domains that legitimately mean different things by the
        same word. Neither direction is a special case of the other.
      </p>
      <p class="body">
        So the reading order below is a movement, not a dependency. Nothing here has to be read first
        for the next thing to make sense, and a work that helps you see another one is not thereby a
        chapter of it.
      </p>

      <h3 class="move-h">Some works sit here and on Foundations</h3>
      <p class="body">
        That is intended. <a href="/read/statistical-bridge">{bridge.label}</a> establishes what must
        exist before inference is possible <em>and</em> governs the passage from governed evidence to
        a claim it licenses. It is on both shelves because a reader might reasonably look for it on
        either, and neither placement is the truer one. The shelves are there to help you find
        things, not to rule on what a work fundamentally is.
      </p>

      <h3 class="move-h">Columna is the next room</h3>
      <p class="body">
        <a href="/columna">Columna</a> is one system built so that these boundaries survive being
        implemented. It is not offered as proof — the argument does not depend on it, and a working
        system is not an argument. It is offered the other way round: here is a principle, here is
        something built to it, and here is enough of its behaviour exposed that you can check.
      </p>

      <hr class="rule" />
      <p class="record">
        Every deposited work, with its current version, date and record, is on
        <a href="/research">the research corpus page</a>. This page is the discussion; that page is
        the record.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .arch { width: min(100% - 3rem, 46rem); margin: 0 auto; padding: 3rem 0 5rem; }
  .kicker { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--ds-mono-sm); letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-faint); margin: 0 0 0.75rem; }
  h1 { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 1.6rem + 2.4vw, 3.4rem);
    line-height: 1.1; margin: 0 0 1.5rem; }
  .lede { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 1rem + 0.6vw, 1.4rem);
    line-height: 1.45; color: var(--ink); margin: 0 0 1.25rem; }
  .body { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0 0 1.1rem; }

  .terrain { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 1rem + 1vw, 1.7rem);
    line-height: 1.35; color: var(--ink); margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem; letter-spacing: 0.01em; }
  .caveat { font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-faint); margin: 0 0 1rem;
    max-width: 38rem; }

  .crossing, .relate { margin-top: 3.5rem; }
  .word { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 1.3rem + 1.2vw, 2.2rem);
    line-height: 1.15; margin: 2rem 0 0.5rem; }
  .question { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.15rem; line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--ink); margin: 0 0 2rem; }

  .moves { display: grid; gap: 2rem; }
  .move-h { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--ds-mono-sm); letter-spacing: 0.07em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-faint); font-weight: 400; margin: 0 0 0.7rem; }

  .opens { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  .opens li { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--ink-soft); border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding: 0.7rem 0; }
  .opens li:last-child { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }

  .held { margin: 1.25rem 0; padding-left: 1.1rem; border-left: 2px solid var(--ink);
    font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.15rem; line-height: 1.4; color: var(--ink); }

  .ways { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  .ways li { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding: 0.7rem 0; }
  .ways li:last-child { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .ways a { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.02rem; }
  .hint { display: block; font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--ink-faint); margin-top: 0.15rem; }

  .record { font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-faint); }
</style>
